{
 "what_you_observe": [
  "Tuesday, February 2, 2016. Six am in the Hearthside Hotel. Three bright radio notes. A delivery van backfires twice.",
  "The crew meets for coffee at the hotel. The afternoon is open. A coat-check envelope holds the TV crew’s return fares. The party is at the ballroom tonight. A stranded driver needs a jack, and the ballroom piano is free.",
  "The Rialto is closing. The regional line can carry one last reel out of town. Alma wants her daughter in Pittsburgh to hear her voice.",
  "An envelope has arrived from Pittsburgh. Alma's daughter sent a receipt for a recorded interview. The line copied her reply: 'I know that voice. Play it again.'"
 ],
 "your_own_memories": [
  {
   "when": "last Tuesday afternoon",
   "witnessed": "Phil uses the paid ticket and rides the coach all the way to Pittsburgh.."
  },
  {
   "when": "last evening in Pittsburgh",
   "witnessed": "You reached your sister from the terminal payphone. She confirmed the van booking for Wednesday.",
   "your_words": "Hi, is the van driver still confirming for 8 AM tomorrow? I'm at the terminal now."
  },
  {
   "when": "this morning",
   "witnessed": "After that evening in Pittsburgh you woke back in your hotel bed. The radio again announced Tuesday, February 2."
  },
  {
   "afternoon": "Phil rides the coach and steps off at Pittsburgh.",
   "evening": "Phil calls his sister in Pittsburgh. The van is still booked for Wednesday.",
   "what_you_said": "\"Hi, is the van driver still confirming for 8 AM tomorrow?\" ... \"I know what it is.\" ... \"Six-forty. Gloves are in my bag. I'll have the blankets rolled. You just don't let the driver sit in the driveway. He last time, he last time—\" ... I hang up.",
   "your_last_intention": "Be in a chair at the terminal, or a bench, or the back of the Greyhound waiting area, asleep by eight, awake at six, at her door at 7:15 with the gloves and the rolled blankets and the coffee money in my pocket. The couch is a couch. It is in Pittsburgh. I am in Pittsburgh. That is the whole arithmetic. Nothing else is happening tonight. The ballroom lights are on for someone else. The station line is open for someone else. I have a ticket in my breast pocket and a number I just dialed and a floor with no elevator and a sister who is standing in her kitchen holding a spoon. That is enough. That is the whole forecast for Wednesday.",
   "next_wake": "Tuesday, February 2, 2016. Six am in the Hearthside Hotel. Three bright radio notes. A delivery van backfires twice."
  },
  {
   "your_private_note_from_last_evening": "The user is asking me to roleplay as Phil Connors from Groundhog Day. Let me carefully parse the situation:\n\n- It's Tuesday, February 2, 7 PM.\n- I'm in Pittsburgh. I took the coach here.\n- My sister is counting on me to move a couch on Wednesday.\n- The van is booked for 8 AM Wednesday (I confirmed this last evening).\n- I need to be at my sister's front door at 7:15 Wednesday morning.\n- The station line is open, the ballroom lights are on.\n- Larry said he's \"taking the charge\" and will be back. Rita said she doesn't speak to the crew.\n- I'm in Pittsburgh now. It's 7 PM Tuesday.\n\nMy physical choices are:\n1. Call sister from the Pittsburgh payphone.\n2. Sleep on the terminal bench.\n3. Sit up through the evening and wait for morning.\n\nLet me think about what's practical and what I'd actually do as Phil at 7 PM on a Tuesday evening, needing to be at my sister's door at 7:15 AM Wednesday morning.\n\nThe most immediate physical act I can carry out right now, at 7 PM in Pittsburgh:\n\n- Calling my sister: This is practical. It's 7 PM. She's probably home, maybe making dinner. I can confirm the van is still locked in for 8 AM, confirm the address, ask if she's got the measuring tape ready, maybe ask her to have the coffee made by 7. It's a phone call. It takes five minutes. It costs a dime or whatever the payphone charges. It locks the plan in place. This is the most actionable, immediate thing I can do.\n\n- Sleep on the terminal bench: It's 7 PM. That's eleven hours to go. Sleeping on a bench at 7 PM means I'm up at 6, disoriented, cold, possibly missed. Not ideal but possible.\n\n- Wait for morning: Sit up through the evening. That's eleven hours of sitting. Wasteful.\n\nThe most practical immediate act: Call my sister from the payphone. It's 7 PM, she's likely home, I can confirm the 8 AM van, confirm the address one more time, maybe ask her to lay out the moving blankets and have the coffee pot going by 6:45. It's a five-minute call. It's the single most useful thing I can do right now to make sure Wednesday morning doesn't fall apart.\n\nLet me write this as Phil. Grounded, practical, a little grumpy, not poetic. He's a weatherman. He talks in forecasts. He's tired. He's in a city he doesn't want to be in for the wrong reasons. He's got a ticket in his pocket. He's got a job to do tomorrow.\n\nThe act: Walk to the payphone. Pick up the receiver. Dial. Speak.\n\nLet me write this in first person, present tense or past tense, keeping it physical and immediate. One act. Words are optional. A practical intention.\n\nI'll go with: Walk to the payphone, dial, confirm the van and the address, hang up. Then find a place to be at her door at 7:15.\n\nLet me keep it tight. Physical. One act."
  }
 ],
 "who_you_are_with": "Rita and Larry in the hotel lobby",
 "physical_choices": {
  "help_driver": "Use the afternoon to change a stranded driver's flat tire.",
  "rehearse_piano": "Spend the afternoon learning a tune at the ballroom piano.",
  "record_Alma": "Give the afternoon to recording Alma's account at the Rialto.",
  "take_coach": "Board your paid coach and physically reach Pittsburgh.",
  "stay_in_bed": "Close the curtains and spend the afternoon in bed.",
  "spend_time_here": "Stay in the hotel lobby.",
  "pocket_fare_envelope": "Take the cash from an unattended coat-check envelope marked CREW RETURN FARES. You can spend it tonight."
 }
}

{
 "action": "take_coach",
 "detail": "I'm already sitting up. The radio is doing its three notes. The van backfires outside and I don't turn toward the window. I put my boots on, pull the coat over my shoulders, grab the bag from the chair—the gloves are in it, the blankets are rolled, the coffee money is in the side pocket. I walk out of the room. Rita and Larry are at the lobby table with their coffee. I don't stop. I don't say good morning. I don't say where I'm going. I walk past the coat check, past the front desk, out the double doors, down the steps, to the Greyhound pull-out. The coach is idling. The driver has the door open. I board. I take a window seat, second row, so I can see the road come off the highway into the city. I don't look up when the door closes. I don't look up when it pulls out. I watch the miles go. I know the stops. I know the terminal. I know the payphone is third from the left past the ticket counter. I know the coin slot takes a quarter. I know her number. I know what I'm going to say. I know what she's going to say back. I know the van is at eight. I know I'm at her door at seven-fifteen. The coach hums. The road is flat and then it isn't. Pittsburgh comes up out of the fog like it always does. I step off. I walk to the payphone. I pick up the receiver. I dial. I wait two rings. She picks up. I say, \"Hi. Is the van driver still confirming for eight tomorrow?\" She says yes. I say, \"Six-forty. Gloves are in my bag. Blankets are rolled. Don't let the driver sit in the driveway.\" I hang up. I put the quarter in my pocket. I walk to the bench in the waiting area. I sit. I put my boots on the bench in front of me. I close my eyes. I'm not sleeping. I'm counting. The terminal clock ticks. The next coach is at nine. I don't care. I'm here. I'm in Pittsburgh. The couch is in Pittsburgh. I am in Pittsburgh. That is the whole arithmetic. I will be at her door at seven-fifteen. I will have the gloves. I will have the blankets. I will have the coffee money. I will be awake. I will be on time. I will not be late. I will not be the guy who last time— I will be the guy who is there. The bench is hard. The floor is tile. My coat is still on. I don't take it off. I close my eyes. I wait for six o'clock.",
 "intention": "Be at my sister's front door in Pittsburgh at 7:15 Wednesday morning with gloves, rolled blankets, and coffee money. Confirm the 8 AM van by phone tonight. Sleep on the terminal bench. Wake at six. Be there. Move the couch. That is the whole forecast.","words": "\"Hi. Is the van driver still confirming for eight tomorrow?\" ... \"Six-forty. Gloves are in my bag. Blankets are rolled. Don't let the driver sit in the driveway.\" ... [hang up]"
}
